    ; `super(…)` gives its object as `object`: it runs the base's constructor on the receiver,
    ; as an initializer, and answers nothing. A constructor the compiler generated runs nothing
    ; then. A method called on the receiver by a constructor, or on the base's part of the
    ; receiver, is the one named, whatever the object's class declares in its place.
